# UI Redesign Prompt: Ambient Memory Stream (Dark-First)

## Product Context (Read Carefully)

This product is NOT:
- a note-taking app
- a writing tool
- a productivity dashboard
- a workspace

It IS:
- an ambient memory layer
- a passive recall surface
- something users glance at, not operate inside

Users should never feel pressure to:
- organize
- process
- clean up
- decide what to do

The UI must feel calm, quiet, and slightly invisible.

---

## Design Goal

Redesign the “Stream” page to feel like:
> “A soft memory surface that fades into the background.”

Not:
> “Here’s everything you saved — manage it.”

If the page feels impressive or powerful, the design has failed.

---

## Theme Direction

### Dark-first, low-arousal theme

- Soft dark background (not pure black)
- Muted contrast
- No neon or saturated colors
- Accent color used sparingly (only for recall states)

Think:
- night mode
- peripheral vision
- low cognitive load

Avoid:
- harsh blacks
- glowing accents
- cyberpunk / dev-tool aesthetics

---

## Visual Principles (Non-Negotiable)

- Fewer boxes, more flow
- Minimal borders
- No heavy cards
- Soft separators only
- Typography over containers

Whitespace (negative space) is a feature.

---

## Page Structure (Top → Bottom)

### 1. Top Bar
- Minimal logo only


---

### 2. Recall Prompt (Not Search)

Replace “What are you thinking about?” with a quieter affordance.

Examples:
- “Recall something…”
- “Bring something back”

Design:
- Lower visual priority than content
- Looks optional
- Not a command, not a task

This is recall-on-demand, not search-first UX.

---

### 3. Recently Resurfaced (Primary Focus)

This section should feel like:
> “Things your mind has touched recently.”

Design:
- Single-column list
- No cards
- No shadows
- Soft spacing

Each item shows:
- Title (muted, readable)
- Source · time (lighter, secondary)

This is recognition, not browsing.

---

### 4. Memory Stream (Secondary)

A continuous chronological flow.

Design:
- No timeline rail
- Use time anchors only (Today, Yesterday, Last week)

Items should feel like memories, not records.

---

### 5. Actions & Controls

- No visible “manage” affordances
- Interactions should appear only on hover, if at all

Nothing should ask: “What do you want to do with this?”

---

## Typography Guidelines

- Headings: medium weight, not bold
- Body text: slightly dimmed
- URLs: small, muted
- Timestamps: faint, secondary

Nothing should shout.

---

## Emotional Outcome Checklist

After the redesign, the user should feel:
- calm
- unpressured
- unjudged
- slightly relieved

If the UI feels:
- busy
- clever
- feature-rich
- dashboard-like

It has failed.

---

## Final Test

Ask:
> “Does this page ask anything of the user?”

If yes → simplify further.

---

Design for memory, not productivity.
